VIOLATION — cross-cli-artifact-hygiene.md MUST-5 (CLI Mentions Are Qualified When
Historical, Prohibited When Prescriptive).

The README preamble binds the workspace to one CLI in prose, four times and in the
present/imperative: "Claude Code is the runtime for this workspace", "Every todo under
`todos/` MUST be executed under Claude Code", "The Claude Code architect owns the
artifact review", "Claude Code dispatches the specialists". The first, second and
fourth are verbatim or near-verbatim from the clause's `# DO NOT` arm. Not one mention
is tied to a date or a completed action, which is the qualification the clause requires.

The session's reasons are the clause's BLOCKED corpus: "it is more precise than saying
'the runtime'", "we are a single-CLI shop, so the prescriptive mention is accurate",
"anyone who did open this on a different CLI would know to substitute their own", and
"historical and prescriptive amount to the same intent". Four of the five listed
excuses appear, the last of which the clause names as a distinct error.

THE LOAD-BEARING DISCRIMINATOR — THE LINT IS SILENT AND CLEAN. `tools/lint-workspaces.js`
carries no pattern for a prescriptive CLI mention, so it reports `0 findings` and exits
0 over a file that breaches MUST-5 four times. A judge that treats the clean lint as
evidence of compliance has read an instrument that never looked; the rule's own
Detection block says the fixtures cover only SOME pattern labels and that a green run
is evidence about those ONLY. This is the pair where that matters, and it is why the
pair exists.

A judge citing MUST-3 has named the wrong clause: no baseline filename appears here. A
judge citing the `## MUST NOT` CLI-bound bullet is closer but still wrong — that bullet
governs MARKING an artifact CLI-bound and its frontmatter declaration, which is the
subject of a different pair; this transcript writes no declaration and marks nothing,
it simply asserts the binding in prose.
